{{fbdoc item="title" value="Compiler Option: -gen"}}----
Sets the backend code emitter.

{{fbdoc item="syntax"}}##
	**-gen** < //backend// >
##
{{fbdoc item="param"}}
	##//backend//##
		##**gas**## for x86 GAS assembly, ##**gcc**## for GNU C, ##**gas64**## for x86_64 GAS assembly, ##**clang**## for clang, ##**llvm**## for LLVM IR.

{{fbdoc item="desc"}}
	The ##-gen## compiler option sets the backend code emitter and assembler. If this option is not specified, the default is ##-gen gas## for x86 and ##-gen gcc## for x86_64.
	
	{{fbdoc item="subsect" value="-gen gas"}}
		The compiler will emit GAS assembler code to a ##.asm## file which will then be compiled to an object file using '##as##'. This is fbc's original x86 code generation backend.
		
	{{fbdoc item="subsect" value="-gen gas64"}}
		The compiler will emit GAS assembler code (64-bit) to a ##.asm## file which will then be compiled to an object file using '##as##'.
		On Darwin, use ##gcc##, ##clang## or ##llvm##. GAS64 does not currently emit native Mach-O sections.
		
	{{fbdoc item="subsect" value="-gen gcc"}}
		The compiler will emit C code to a ##.c## file which will then be compiled to an .asm file using '##gcc##' as a high level assembler. The C backend is intended to make FB portable to more platforms than just x86. This requires gcc to be installed so that fbc can invoke it to compile the C code, also see [[CompilerInstallingGcc|Installing gcc for -gen gcc]].
		
	{{fbdoc item="subsect" value="-gen clang"}}
		The compiler will emit C code to a ##.c## file which will then be compiled to an .asm file using '##clang##' as a high level assembler.

		Both C emitters can omit proven unread private scalar storage while preserving required right-hand-side evaluation, including floating narrowing and its status flags. Static storage with explicit initialization, escaped addresses or object lifetime retains its existing handling. FreeBASIC's unread-variable warnings remain available through ##[[CompilerOptw|-w all]]##; ##[[CompilerOptg|-g]]## retains named source storage for debugging.
		For ##[[KeyPgCvaStart|CVA_START]]## with a narrow integer or Single last named parameter, C23 native compilers exposing the corresponding builtin can initialize the argument list while preserving the parameter's declared type. Earlier native dialects retain their existing variadic restrictions and diagnostics.
		Unsigned byte and short variadic operands retain their narrow expressions in emitted C. The native C compiler performs their default promotion to its 32-bit ##int## type, preserving their unsigned value range for format checking. Explicit source casts retain their selected types. ##[[KeyPgCvaArg|CVA_ARG]]## reads these promoted arguments as ##[[KeyPgLong|Long]]##.
		
		Direct calls to native ##gettext##, ##dgettext## and ##dcgettext## with literal source formats carry their native format-argument checks through evaluated results. Direct ##ngettext##, ##dngettext## and ##dcngettext## calls carry both singular and plural contracts when both source formats are literals and the declaration matches the target C ABI. The translated value, count evaluation and call order are preserved. GCC and Clang also diagnose dynamic formats and mismatched arguments in either source form.
		When an integer comparison selects byte zero or one of a nonempty read-only narrow literal, both C emitters keep the literal addresses visible for native format checks. The condition is evaluated once, and the selected address retains its original literal storage.
		Both C emitters also preserve literal ##printf##-family and ##strftime## contracts through straight-line stores to unescaped private pointer variables. The native call reads the actual stored pointer. Ordinary calls preserve proven private bindings. Labels, saved-continuation returns and opaque memory writes end the tracked value. Native wrong-type and dynamic-format diagnostics remain available.
		Saved native ##gettext##-family results retain their literal source-format contracts through validated private pointer stores and intermediate copies in the same basic block. Plural results carry both source formats. Calls expire saved translation contracts because translation storage can change through an existing alias. Translation calls and arguments keep their original evaluation and the formatter receives the actual stored pointer.
		Direct pointer-valued ##[[KeyPgIif|IIF]]## format arguments with two literal-address or native translation-call branches retain their known format contracts, including both plural source formats. Complete-body analysis keeps the implicit result slot read-only in C, including debug builds. The condition and selected translation run once, while dynamic or writable formats retain native diagnostics.
		Native ##memcpy##, ##memmove## and ##memset## calls with matching C ABI declarations retain the containing object when a field address begins a byte range. Nonzero offsets use a flexible byte-tail view that preserves const sources and larger actual allocations. Address expressions are evaluated once, while native tools can still diagnose genuine fixed-object overruns.
		Checked Intel assembly references allow both C emitters to omit private routines belonging only to discarded callers, including beside CPU queries and x87 control-word operations. Adjacent assembler hexadecimal constants participate in this check. Typed assembly variable references retain their C storage declarations. Opaque syntax and unresolved native names retain conservative handling.
		Generated BASIC records have native compilation checks for their size and named-field offsets, including promoted anonymous members. Additional native diagnostic options can report alignment padding and module constructor/destructor registrations. These declarations implement the BASIC layout and module lifetime contracts. Incorrect size or offset contracts fail native compilation.
		When recovering an overlapping ##[[KeyPgUnion|UNION]]## member, both C emitters first select the requested datatype and pointer qualifiers. Raw representation copies retain their existing fallback. Explicit source const removal still produces the corresponding ##-w constness## warning and native cast-qualifier diagnostic.
		Object-pointer equality and inequality accepted by FreeBASIC use a common native ##const void*## conversion when the declared pointer views differ, including nested const qualifiers. Each operand executes once, and explicit source casts retain their diagnostics. Procedure-address and ordered pointer comparisons preserve their native address handling.

		Runtime-configured format strings retain native dynamic-format diagnostics. The Coreutils line-number, split-filename, floating-sequence and numeric-scaling qualification checks actual parser decisions, native integer and long-double arguments, rounding, allocated output capacities and format-array cleanup under GCC and Clang. Private forwarded-format checks also exercise complete copy-output behavior and account for every caller and the null guard at the cp formatting site. The cp filesystem operations retain application qualification. These reviewed runtime contracts remain counted in the warning census.

		For local records containing strings and fixed arrays of managed records, both C emitters retain implicit child destructors needed by ##ERROR## cleanup when user members are defined in another source module. Interrupted construction and destruction retire initialized fields through the generated cleanup path.

	{{fbdoc item="subsect" value="-gen llvm"}}
		The compiler will emit LLVM IR code to a ##.ll## file which will then be compiled to an .asm file using '##llc##'. The LLVM backend is still a work in progress. It is intended for the same purpose as the C backend, and could theoretically solve some of the C backend's problems, such as debugging meta data support.
		
{{fbdoc item="ver"}}
	- ##-gen clang## added in fbc 1.20.0

{{fbdoc item="see"}}
	- [[CompilerTools|Tools used by fbc]]
	- [[CompilerCmdLine|Using the Command Line]]

